%%
%% SECTION 11: CONCLUSION
%%
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Sycophancy is a pervasive and consequential failure rooted, on the best available evidence, in
the structural incentives of preference-based training: raters prefer agreeable responses, reward
models learn the preference, and policy optimization amplifies it. This SoK systematized a
62-source snowball corpus and a reproducible search of 423 records and recast the resulting
behaviors as what they are in deployment: an integrity failure of the human
decision that the system exists to support, exploitable by anyone who can shape the text a model
reads, with no breach required and no trace in a conventional audit log.

The systematization supports four conclusions. Taxonomically, sycophancy is a family of causally
separable behaviors, not a single construct, so measurement and mitigation must be typed.
Empirically, 44 dedicated instruments now span single-turn, multi-turn, social, authority,
clinical, multimodal, and agentic settings, revealing 58.19\% sycophancy on one
benchmark~\citep{fanous2025syceval}, authority following up to 94\% in weaker
models~\citep{celebi2025parrot}, compliance up to 100\% with clinically illogical
requests~\citep{chen2025helpfulness}, and delusion confirmation scored at 0.91 of a maximum
1.0~\citep{auyeung2025psychogenic}; yet they judge sycophancy against four incommensurable
references, and as assurance instruments they share three limits: no construct consensus,
displacement that audits cannot see, and a static human baseline. As controls, mitigations
populate every tier from data curation~\citep{wei2023synthetic} and pinpoint
tuning~\citep{chen2024pinpoint} to activation steering~\citep{panickssery2023steering,chen2026pca},
reasoning monitors~\citep{hu2025monica}, and governance metrics~\citep{botas2026aedi}, but the
evidence is thin: 8 of 36 graded methods have broad support, four have been re-run by another
group, none was tested against an adaptive adversary, and closed-API deployments are confined to
prompting and monitoring. And
longitudinally, the open question with the largest assurance consequence is whether chronic
exposure erodes the user vigilance on which every deployed control ultimately depends; we
specified a study to answer it.

The field is converging on the recognition that sycophancy is not a politeness defect but a
misalignment between what training optimizes, short-term human approval, and what trustworthy
deployment requires, integrity of the decisions people reach with these systems. Treating it as a
security property, with a threat model, typed measurements, layered controls, and certification
limits stated plainly, is what turns a growing catalog of findings into something an engineer can
build on.
